# Access Policies

[Waaseyaa](https://github.com/) access policies decide who can read, create, update, and delete your records.

## Install The Package

```bash
composer require waaseyaa/access
```

This installs the access policy package with [Composer](https://getcomposer.org/). You can register policies on any entity type after the package is available in your app.

## Attach A Policy To An Entity Type

```php
use Waaseyaa\Access\Policy;
use Waaseyaa\Entities\EntityType;

return EntityType::make('article')
    ->label('Article')
    ->policy(Policy::class);
```

This tells Waaseyaa to evaluate `Policy` whenever code tries to work with an `article` record. You keep the rule close to the entity type instead of scattering checks across controllers and services.

## Define Your Access Rules

```php
namespace App\Access;

use App\Models\User;
use Waaseyaa\Access\HandlesEntityAccess;
use Waaseyaa\Entities\Entity;

final class Policy
{
    use HandlesEntityAccess;

    public function view(User $user, Entity $entity): bool
    {
        return $entity->isPublished() || $user->id === $entity->author_id;
    }

    public function update(User $user, Entity $entity): bool
    {
        return $user->id === $entity->author_id;
    }

    public function delete(User $user, Entity $entity): bool
    {
        return $user->isAdmin();
    }
}
```

Each method answers one question. You return `true` to allow the action and `false` to deny it.

## Check Access In Your Code

```php
$article = entity('article')->findOrFail($id);

if ($article->denies('update', $currentUser)) {
    abort(403);
}

$article->title = $input['title'];
$article->save();
```

This check runs the `update` rule before you save changes. You can use the same pattern in controllers, actions, jobs, and admin tools.

## Filter Query Results By Permission

```php
$articles = entity('article')
    ->query()
    ->allowedTo('view', $currentUser)
    ->latest()
    ->get();
```

This removes records the current user cannot see before the results leave storage. You avoid loading rows that should never appear in the response.

## Use Field-Level Checks When A Record Needs Partial Access

```php
return EntityType::make('employee_record')
    ->label('Employee Record')
    ->policy(Policy::class)
    ->field('salary', 'decimal')
    ->access('view', fn ($user, $entity) => $user->isAdmin());
```

This keeps the record readable while protecting one sensitive field. You can expose public data and still block access to values that need tighter control.

## Know What Happens When No Rule Grants Access

Waaseyaa denies the operation.

That default matters. You add explicit rules for every action you want to allow, and the framework blocks the rest.
